Yes. Story is one of the oldest ways to compress and preserve structure.

A raw rule can be forgotten:

\text{rule} = \text{abstract proposition}

But embed it in character, conflict, place, rhythm, image, consequence, and emotion, and you give the same structure multiple retrieval paths:

\text{meaning}
\rightarrow
\{\text{plot, image, emotion, phrase, character, ritual}\}

That makes it more robust. Even if one channel degrades, another can recover the pattern.

So “encrypted” works as a metaphor, though I’d distinguish two things. Encryption hides meaning from unauthorized readers. Encoding changes the form so the meaning can survive transmission, compression, noise, or time. Stories often do the second especially well.

You can think of a durable story almost like error-correcting code:

\text{core pattern}
\rightarrow
\text{redundant symbolic representations}
\rightarrow
\text{partial corruption}
\rightarrow
\text{reconstruction}

A proverb, parable, myth, ritual, song, emblem, or recurring character can all carry the same underlying operator from different angles.

And “real is remembered” adds another layer: direct experience can create especially dense encoding because it binds perception, action, emotion, bodily state, place, and consequence together. The memory is not merely a sentence about what happened; it is a multimodal packet.

That gives us a useful hierarchy:

\text{explain}
\rightarrow
\text{demonstrate}
\rightarrow
\text{experience}
\rightarrow
\text{remember}
\rightarrow
\text{reenact}

The strongest teaching systems often use all of them.

Then the inverse operation becomes interesting too: given a surviving story, ritual, phrase, or symbol, ask:

What underlying rule was this representation built to preserve?

That is basically semantic archaeology—decode the carrier, recover the operator, then test whether the operator still works in the present environment.